@begin(section)

@title(Overview)

@image[src=assets/zick.png]()

zick is a package manager for project-level source-code repositories:
it installs zip archives into a project tree, tracks which files each
package owns, and verifies the result on disk.  The name is a backronym
— **Zip files In Concert** — and the idea is to be the "RPM" or "DEB"
for a project rather than for a whole machine.

The mental model is small:

@begin(list)
@item(A project is marked by a @c(.zick-db) directory, found by walking
up from where you run zick.  Everything zick knows about a project
lives under that directory.)
@item(Packages are plain zip archives served over HTTP(S).  @c(zick add)
downloads one, unpacks it into the project tree, and records the result.)
@item(The store — an NRDL document of installed packages and the files
they own, with sizes and SHA-256 checksums — lives in the @c(.zick-db)
directory.  It is the source of truth for what is installed.)
@item(@c(zick verify) checks the files on disk against the store, so a
@c(git clean), a bad deploy, or a partial restore can't silently break
your environment.)
@end(list)

zick is built for two audiences:

@begin(list)
@item(Dev machines, where you set up the dependencies you need to do
programming work — a toolchain bundle, a set of prebuilt libraries, a
data pack.)
@item(CI/CD, where a checkout plus @c(zick add) plus @c(zick verify) is
a reproducible, auditable install step.)
@end(list)

@begin(section)
@title(How zick Fits with the Resolver)

zick pairs well with @link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/dsolv"](dsolv),
a generic dependency resolver.  They are independent tools built to be
used together:

@begin(list)
@item(dsolv reads a repository of cards describing versions and
requirements, and tells you the exact set of versions a build needs —
returning the URLs of the files to download.)
@item(zick takes those URLs, downloads the archives, unpacks them into
the project tree, tracks the files it owns, and verifies them.)
@end(list)

dsolv answers "what do we need?"; zick answers "make it so, and
check."  This manual covers zick on its own; the pairing is shown in
the @link[uri="a-longer-example.html"](longer example) — where a
script pipes dsolv's install-order JSON straight into @c(zick add) —
and the @link[uri="architecture.html"](architecture) pages.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(What zick Does Not Do)

zick is deliberately small:

@begin(list)
@item(It does not resolve versions.  You give @c(zick add) a name, a
version, and a URL; zick installs exactly that.  Version selection is
the resolver's job, not zick's.)
@item(It does not build or compile anything.  Archives are unpacked as
they are; publishing is just uploading a zip.)
@item(It does not manage your whole machine.  zick installs into one
project tree, and its ownership database lives in that project.)
@end(list)

@end(section)

@end(section)
